|passage=''Blue'' was released, and as Redford had predicted, it was a '''dog'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Ronald L. Davis|title=Duke: The Life and Image of John Wayne
|passage="When ''The Alamo'' was coming out, the word of mouth on it was that it was a '''dog'''," Chase said.}}
# {{lb|en|firearms|archaic}} A [[cock]], as of a [[gun]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1832|title=The Edinburgh Encyclopaedia|volume=10|page=164
|passage=To this succeeded the ''[[snaphance|Snaplance]]'' {{sic}}, in which a motion was given to the '''dog''', or cock, and a movable plate of steel, called the frizel, or hammer, was placed vertically above the pan to receive the action of the flint.}}
# {{lb|en|preceded by definite article}} A [[dance]] having a brief [[vogue]] in the 1960s in which the actions of a dog were [[mimic]]ked.
#* {{quote-song|en|lyricist={{w|Ray Charles}}|title={{w|What'd I Say}}|artist={{w|Elvis Presley}}|year=1964|text=Hmm, see the girl with the red dress on<br>She can do the '''dog''' all night long.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|lyricist={{w|Thomas Coman}} and Sylvester Stewart ({{w|Sly Stone}})|title={{w|C'mon and Swim}}|artist={{w|Bobby Freeman}}|year=1964|text=Just like the '''dog''' but not so low<br> Like the hully gully but not so slow<br> Now baby swim, baby do the swim.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Trudy Baker; Rachel Jones; {{w|Donald Bain (writer)|Donald Bain}} (uncredited)|title={{w|Coffee, Tea or Me?|Coffee, Tea, or Me?: The Uninhibited Memoirs of Two Airline Stewardesses}}|year=1967|publisher=Bantam Books|location=New York|page=173|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/coffeeteaormeuni00bake/page/173/mode/1up|passage=We had a lovely dance—luckily it was a slow number. I don't think Mr. Lincoln would have been up to the '''dog''' or a fast twist.}}

